# An ALLOW-list, not a deny-list: exclude everything, then name the handful of
# things the build actually reads.
#
# The two failure modes are not symmetric. A deny-list that forgets an entry
# ships that entry — and what is sitting in this directory right now is a live
# .env (real connection strings, real internal keys) and .data/, which holds
# real customer documents the filesystem blob store wrote. Either one baked into
# a layer is a disclosure that survives every later fix, because layers are
# immutable and get pushed to a registry. An allow-list that forgets an entry
# breaks the build, loudly, on the first push. Loud beats silent.
#
# .env deserves its own sentence. config.ts does `import "dotenv/config"`, so a
# .env present in the image is not inert — dotenv fills in any key the
# environment did not set. A deployment that forgot BIB_APP_DATABASE_URL would
# not fail to boot; it would boot cleanly against whatever a developer's laptop
# was pointed at.
*

# Build inputs, in the order the Dockerfile copies them.
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!tsconfig.json
!tsup.config.ts
!src

# Runtime input. migrate reads these .up.sql files from disk when the job runs,
# so unlike everything else under src/ they are not compiled into anything and
# the image has to carry them. 1.1 MB, and the one large directory here that
# has to survive.
!migrations

# Re-excluded from inside the allowed trees. `*` above only matches top-level
# entries, so anything nested under src/ or migrations/ needs naming again.
# Nothing secret lives there today; these are here so that nothing has to.
**/.DS_Store
**/node_modules
**/.env
**/.env.*
